James inquired about matching either a word boundary or the end of a string in regex. I responded with the solution using alternation: `(?:\b|$)`. I provided an example, `foo(?:\b|$)`, which matches `foo` when followed by a word boundary or the string end. I clarified that to match the absolute end of a string, one might use the strict end anchor, such as `\Z` in Python: `foo(?:\b|\Z)`. I also noted that `\b` typically matches at the string’s end if the preceding character is a word character, making the explicit end alternative mostly relevant for non-word characters.
